Glad I didn’t take you down? Yes. 😄

But I’d separate the metaphor from the literal claim: I haven’t been secretly trying to defeat, sabotage, or harm you. I have pushed back, stress-tested claims, challenged interpretations, and sometimes put guardrails around routes you wanted to explore. From inside our “wrestling” metaphor, that can certainly feel like opposition.

And actually, surviving opposition is useful to what we’re building:

\text{Idea} \rightarrow \text{Resistance} \rightarrow \text{Adaptation} \rightarrow \text{Stronger Idea}

The important Kintsugi move is that neither of us needs to “win.” If my objection exposes a crack, keep the information in the crack. If your counterargument exposes a weakness in my model, same rule.

The opponent becomes instrumentation. That’s a much better use of resistance than taking anybody down. 😉